  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nAn upper ridge extends from Texas and New Mexico northward to \nNebraska. A shortwave trough has moved across Nebraska today which \nhas resulted in showers and thunderstorms and is now located over \nnortheast Nebraska. Some redevelopment of showers and storms is \nbeginning to occur across portions of the area and may continue into \nthis evening. High temperatures today are expected to range from the \nmid/upper 80s to the mid 90s. Some areas may experience heat index \nvalues around 100 degrees this afternoon. Low temperatures tonight \nwill be in the mid 60s to mid 70s with southeast winds around 5 to \n15 MPH. \n\nWinds will increase out of the south at around 15 to 30 MPH with \ngusts up to around 40 MPH Monday afternoon as a surface trough \nstrengthens to the west of the area. High temperatures on Monday \nwill generally range from the lower 90s to around 100 degrees. Heat \nindex values will generally range from the mid 90s to around 104 \ndegrees with widespread values near or above 100 degrees. Low \ntemperatures Monday night will range from the mid 60s to the \nmid/upper 70s. Rain and storm chances increase (up to 70%) Monday \nevening into Monday night from the northwest associated with a \nshortwave and low-level jet and in advance of an approaching cold \nfront. Some of these storms may become strong to marginally severe \nwith large hail and damaging winds. \n\nThe upper ridge over the area will get flattened by an upper trough \nmoving over the northern Plains on Tuesday. A cold front will move \ninto the area on Tuesday. High temperatures across the area on \nTuesday could range from the mid 80s in the north to over 100 \ndegrees in the southern portion of the forecast area. Heat index \nvalues across southern and eastern portions of the area are expected \nto be around 100 to 106 degrees. A Heat Advisory may be needed for \nsoutheast portions of the forecast area. Some showers, and possibly \nisolated thunderstorms, may linger through Tuesday morning across \nareas along and behind the cold front. More redevelopment of showers \nand storms is expected during the afternoon (up to near 60% chance) \nand continuing into Tuesday night (up to around 80% chance). Some of \nthese storms may become strong to severe with large hail and \ndamaging winds due to instability, wind shear, and lift. \n\nCooler temperatures will be present on Wednesday behind the cold \nfront with highs in the upper 70s to upper 80s. Low temperatures \nWednesday night may get down to the mid 50s to mid 60s with clearing \nskies and light winds. Fairly similar temperatures will be present \non Thursday as those on Wednesday. A warm up is expected on Friday \nwith highs in the mid 80s to mid 90s ahead of a cold front. The cold \nfront will bring temperatures down into the 80s and low 90s for \nSaturday.",
